<p align=3D"CENTER">
<b>BERKSHIRE HATHAWAY INC.</b>
</p>

<pre>

<b>To the Shareholders of Berkshire Hathaway Inc.:</b>


     Our gain in net worth during 1995 was $5.3 billion, or=20
45.0%.  Per-share book value grew by a little less, 43.1%,=20
because we paid stock for two acquisitions, increasing our shares=20
outstanding by 1.3%.  Over the last 31 years (that is, since=20
present management took over) per-share book value has grown from=20
$19 to $14,426, or at a rate of 23.6% compounded annually.

     There's no reason to do handsprings over 1995's gains.  This=20
was a year in which any fool could make a bundle in the stock=20
market.  And we did.  To paraphrase President Kennedy, a rising=20
tide lifts all yachts.

     Putting aside the financial results, there was plenty of=20
good news at Berkshire last year:  We negotiated three=20
acquisitions of exactly the type we desire.  Two of these,=20
Helzberg's Diamond Shops and R.C. Willey Home Furnishings, are=20
included in our 1995 financial statements, while our largest=20
transaction, the purchase of GEICO, closed immediately after the=20
end of the year.  (I'll tell you more about all three=20
acquisitions later in the report.)

     These new subsidiaries roughly double our revenues.  Even=20
so, the acquisitions neither materially increased our shares=20
outstanding nor our debt.  And, though these three operations=20
employ over 11,000 people, our headquarters staff grew only from=20
11 to 12.  (No sense going crazy.)

     Charlie Munger, Berkshire's Vice Chairman and my partner,=20
and I want to build a collection of companies - both wholly- and=20
partly-owned - that have excellent economic characteristics and=20
that are run by outstanding managers.  Our favorite acquisition=20
is the negotiated transaction that allows us to purchase 100% of=20
such a business at a fair price.  But we are almost as happy when=20
the stock market offers us the chance to buy a modest percentage=20
of an outstanding business at a pro-rata price well below what it=20
would take to buy 100%.  This double-barrelled approach -=20
purchases of entire businesses through negotiation or purchases=20
of part-interests through the stock market - gives us an=20
important advantage over capital-allocators who stick to a single=20
course.  Woody Allen once explained why eclecticism works:  "The=20
real advantage of being bisexual is that it doubles your chances=20
for a date on Saturday night."

     Over the years, we've been Woody-like in our thinking,=20
attempting to increase our marketable investments in wonderful=20
businesses, while simultaneously trying to buy similar businesses=20
in their entirety.  The following table illustrates our progress=20
on both fronts.  In the tabulation, we show the marketable=20
securities owned per share of Berkshire at ten-year intervals.  A=20
second column lists our per-share operating earnings (before=20
taxes and purchase-price adjustments but after interest and=20
corporate overhead) from all other activities.  In other words,=20
the second column shows what we earned excluding the dividends,=20
interest and capital gains that we realized from investments. =20
Purchase-price accounting adjustments are ignored for reasons we=20
have explained at length in previous reports and which, as an act=20
of mercy, we won't repeat.  (We'll be glad to send masochists the=20
earlier explanations, however.)

