     These results have not sprung from some master plan that we=20
concocted in 1965.  In a general way, we knew then what we hoped=20
to accomplish but had no idea what specific opportunities might=20
make it possible.  Today we remain similarly unstructured:  Over=20
time, we expect to improve the figures in both columns but have=20
no road map to tell us how that will come about.

     We proceed with two advantages:  First, our operating=20
managers are outstanding and, in most cases, have an unusually=20
strong attachment to Berkshire.  Second, Charlie and I have had=20
considerable experience in allocating capital and try to go at=20
that job rationally and objectively.  The giant disadvantage we=20
face is size:  In the early years, we needed only good ideas, but=20
now we need good <i>big</i> ideas.  Unfortunately, the difficulty of=20
finding these grows in direct proportion to our financial=20
success, a problem that increasingly erodes our strengths.

     I will have more to say about Berkshire's prospects later in=20
this report, when I discuss our proposed recapitalization.

<b>Acquisitions</b> =20

     It may seem strange that we exult over a year in which we=20
made three acquisitions, given that we have regularly used these=20
pages to question the acquisition activities of most managers. =20
Rest assured, Charlie and I haven't lost our skepticism:  We=20
believe most deals do damage to the shareholders of the acquiring=20
company.  Too often, the words from <i>HMS Pinafore</i> apply:  "Things=20
are seldom what they seem, skim milk masquerades as cream." =20
Specifically, sellers and their representatives invariably=20
present financial projections having more entertainment value=20
than educational value.  In the production of rosy scenarios,=20
Wall Street can hold its own against Washington.

     In any case, why potential buyers even look at projections=20
prepared by sellers baffles me.  Charlie and I never give them a=20
glance, but instead keep in mind the story of the man with an=20
ailing horse.  Visiting the vet, he said:  "Can you help me? =20
Sometimes my horse walks just fine and sometimes he limps."  The=20
vet's reply was pointed:  "No problem - when he's walking fine,=20
sell him."  In the world of mergers and acquisitions, that horse=20
would be peddled as Secretariat.

     At Berkshire, we have all the difficulties in perceiving the=20
future that other acquisition-minded companies do.  Like they=20
also, we face the inherent problem that the seller of a business=20
practically always knows far more about it than the buyer and=20
also picks the time of sale - a time when the business is likely=20
to be walking "just fine."

     Even so, we do have a few advantages, perhaps the greatest=20
being that we <i>don't</i> have a strategic plan.  Thus we feel no need=20
to proceed in an ordained direction (a course leading almost=20
invariably to silly purchase prices) but can instead simply=20
decide what makes sense for our owners.  In doing that, we always=20
mentally compare any move we are contemplating with dozens of=20
other opportunities open to us, including the purchase of small=20
pieces of the best businesses in the world via the stock market.=20
Our practice of making this comparison - acquisitions against=20
passive investments - is a discipline that managers focused=20
simply on expansion seldom use.

     Talking to <i>Time Magazine</i> a few years back, Peter Drucker got=20
to the heart of things:  "I will tell you a secret: Dealmaking=20
beats working.  Dealmaking is exciting and fun, and working is=20
grubby.  Running anything is primarily an enormous amount of=20
grubby detail work . . . dealmaking is romantic, sexy.  That's=20
why you have deals that make no sense."

     In making acquisitions, we have a further advantage:  As=20
payment, we can offer sellers a stock backed by an extraordinary=20
collection of outstanding businesses.  An individual or a family=20
wishing to dispose of a single fine business, but also wishing to=20
defer personal taxes indefinitely, is apt to find Berkshire stock=20
a particularly comfortable holding.  I believe, in fact, that=20
this calculus played an important part in the two acquisitions=20
for which we paid shares in 1995.

     Beyond that, sellers sometimes care about placing their=20
companies in a corporate home that will both endure and provide=20
pleasant, productive working conditions for their managers.  Here=20
again, Berkshire offers something special.  Our managers operate=20
with extraordinary autonomy.  Additionally, our ownership=20
structure enables sellers to know that when I say we are buying=20
to keep, the promise means something.  For our part, we like=20
dealing with owners who care what happens to their companies and=20
people.  A buyer is likely to find fewer unpleasant surprises=20
dealing with that type of seller than with one simply auctioning=20
off his business.

     In addition to the foregoing being an explanation of our=20
acquisition style, it is, of course, a not-so-subtle sales pitch.=20
If you own or represent a business earning $25 million or more=20
before tax, and it fits the criteria listed on page 23, just=20
give me a call.  Our discussion will be confidential.  And if you=20
aren't interested now, file our proposition in the back of your=20
mind:  We are never going to lose our appetite for buying=20
companies with good economics and excellent management.

     Concluding this little dissertation on acquisitions, I can't=20
resist repeating a tale told me last year by a corporate=20
executive.  The business he grew up in was a fine one, with a=20
long-time record of leadership in its industry.  Its main=20
product, however, was distressingly glamorless.  So several=20
decades ago, the company hired a management consultant who -=20
naturally - advised diversification, the then-current fad. =20
("Focus" was not yet in style.)  Before long, the company=20
acquired a number of businesses, each after the consulting firm=20
had gone through a long - and expensive - acquisition study.  And=20
the outcome?  Said the executive sadly, "When we started, we were=20
getting 100% of our earnings from the original business.  After=20
ten years, we were getting 150%."

<b>Helzberg's Diamond Shops</b>

     A few years back, management consultants popularized a=20
technique called "management by walking around" (MBWA).  At=20
Berkshire, we've instituted ABWA (acquisitions by walking=20
around).

     In May 1994, a week or so after the Annual Meeting, I was=20
crossing the street at 58th and Fifth Avenue in New York, when a=20
woman called out my name.  I listened as she told me she'd been=20
to, and had enjoyed, the Annual Meeting.  A few seconds later, a=20
man who'd heard the woman stop me did so as well.  He turned out=20
to be Barnett Helzberg, Jr., who owned four shares of Berkshire=20
and had also been at our meeting.

     In our few minutes of conversation, Barnett said he had a=20
business we might be interested in.  When people say that, it=20
usually turns out they have a lemonade stand - with potential, of=20
course, to quickly grow into the next Microsoft.  So I simply=20
asked Barnett to send me particulars.  That, I thought to myself.=20
will be the end of that.

     Not long after, Barnett sent me the financial statements of=20
Helzberg's Diamond Shops.  The company had been started by his=20
grandfather in 1915 from a single store in Kansas City and had=20
developed by the time we met into a group with 134 stores in 23=20
states.  Sales had grown from $10 million in 1974 to $53 million=20
in 1984 and $282 million in 1994.  We weren't talking lemonade=20
stands.

     Barnett, then 60, loved the business but also wanted to feel=20
free of it.  In 1988, as a step in that direction, he had brought=20
in Jeff Comment, formerly President of Wanamaker's, to help him=20
run things.  The hiring of Jeff turned out to be a homerun, but=20
Barnett still found that he couldn't shake a feeling of ultimate=20
responsibility.  Additionally, he owned a valuable asset that was=20
subject to the vagaries of a single, very competitive industry,=20
and he thought it prudent to diversify his family's holdings.

     Berkshire was made to order for him.  It took us awhile to=20
get together on price, but there was never any question in my=20
mind that, first, Helzberg's was the kind of business that we=20
wanted to own and, second, Jeff was our kind of manager.  In=20
fact, we would not have bought the business if Jeff had not been=20
there to run it.  Buying a retailer without good management is=20
like buying the Eiffel Tower without an elevator.

     We completed the Helzberg purchase in 1995 by means of a=20
tax-free exchange of stock, the only kind of transaction that=20
interested Barnett.  Though he was certainly under no obligation=20
to do so, Barnett shared a meaningful part of his proceeds from=20
the sale with a large number of his associates.  When someone=20
behaves that generously, you know you are going to be treated=20
right as a buyer.

     The average Helzberg's store has annual sales of about $2=20
million, far more than competitors operating similarly-sized=20
stores achieve.  This superior per-store productivity is the key=20
to Helzberg's excellent profits.  If the company continues its=20
first-rate performance - and we believe it will - it could grow=20
rather quickly to several times its present size.

     Helzberg's, it should be added, is an entirely different=20
sort of operation from Borsheim's, our Omaha jewelry business,=20
and the two companies will operate independently of each other. =20
Borsheim's had an excellent year in 1995, with sales up 11.7%. =20
Susan Jacques, its 36-year-old CEO, had an even better year,=20
giving birth to her second son at the start of the Christmas=20
season.  Susan has proved to be a terrific leader in the two=20
years since her promotion.

<b>R.C. Willey Home Furnishings</b> =20

     It was Nebraska Furniture Mart's Irv Blumkin who did the=20
walking around in the case of R.C. Willey, long the leading home=20
furnishings business in Utah.  Over the years, Irv had told me=20
about the strengths of that company.  And he had also told Bill=20
Child, CEO of R.C. Willey, how pleased the Blumkin family had=20
been with its Berkshire relationship.  So in early 1995, Bill=20
mentioned to Irv that for estate tax and diversification reasons,=20
he and the other owners of R.C. Willey might be interested in=20
selling.

     From that point forward, things could not have been simpler.=20
Bill sent me some figures, and I wrote him a letter indicating=20
my idea of value.  We quickly agreed on a number, and found our=20
personal chemistry to be perfect.  By mid-year, the merger was=20
completed.

     R.C. Willey is an amazing story.  Bill took over the=20
business from his father-in-law in 1954 when sales were about=20
$250,000.  From this tiny base, Bill employed Mae West's=20
philosophy:  "It's not what you've got - it's what you do with=20
what you've got."  Aided by his brother, Sheldon, Bill has built=20
the company to its 1995 sales volume of $257 million, and it now=20
accounts for over 50% of the furniture business in Utah.  Like=20
Nebraska Furniture Mart, R.C. Willey sells appliances,=20
electronics, computers and carpets in addition to furniture. =20
Both companies have about the same sales volume, but NFM gets all=20
of its business from one complex in Omaha, whereas R.C. Willey=20
will open its sixth major store in the next few months.

     Retailing is a tough business.  During my investment career,=20
I have watched a large number of retailers enjoy terrific growth=20
and superb returns on equity for a period, and then suddenly=20
nosedive, often all the way into bankruptcy.  This shooting-star=20
phenomenon is far more common in retailing than it is in=20
manufacturing or service businesses.  In part, this is because a=20
retailer must stay smart, day after day.  Your competitor is=20
always copying and then topping whatever you do.  Shoppers are=20
meanwhile beckoned in every conceivable way to try a stream of=20
new merchants.  In retailing, to coast is to fail.

     In contrast to this have-to-be-smart-every-day business,=20
there is what I call the have-to-be-smart-<i>once</i> business.  For=20
example, if you were smart enough to buy a network TV station=20
very early in the game, you could put in a shiftless and backward=20
nephew to run things, and the business would still do well for=20
decades.  You'd do far better, of course, if you put in Tom=20
Murphy, but you could stay comfortably in the black without him.=20
For a retailer, hiring that nephew would be an express ticket to=20
bankruptcy.

     The two retailing businesses we purchased this year are=20
blessed with terrific managers who love to compete and have done=20
so successfully for decades.  Like the CEOs of our other=20
operating units, they will operate autonomously:  We want them to=20
feel that the businesses they run are <i>theirs.</i>  This means no=20
second-guessing by Charlie and me.  We avoid the attitude of the=20
alumnus whose message to the football coach is "I'm 100% with you=20
- win or tie."  Our basic goal as an owner is to behave with our=20
managers as we like our owners to behave with us.

     As we add more operations, I'm sometimes asked how many=20
people I can handle reporting to me.  My answer to that is=20
simple:  If I have one person reporting to me and he is a lemon,=20
that's one too many, and if I have managers like those we now=20
have, the number can be almost unlimited.  We are lucky to have=20
Bill and Sheldon associated with us, and we hope that we can=20
acquire other businesses that bring with them managers of similar=20
caliber.

<b>GEICO Corporation</b>

     Right after yearend, we completed the purchase of 100% of=20
GEICO, the seventh largest auto insurer in the United States,=20
with about 3.7 million cars insured.  I've had a 45-year=20
association with GEICO, and though the story has been told=20
before, it's worth a short recap here.

     I attended Columbia University's business school in 1950-51,=20
not because I cared about the degree it offered, but because I=20
wanted to study under Ben Graham, then teaching there.  The time=20
I spent in Ben's classes was a personal high, and quickly induced=20
me to learn all I could about my hero.  I turned first to <i>Who's=20
Who in America,</i> finding there, among other things, that Ben was=20
Chairman of Government Employees Insurance Company, to me an=20
unknown company in an unfamiliar industry.

     A librarian next referred me to Best's Fire and Casualty=20
insurance manual, where I learned that GEICO was based in=20
Washington, DC.  So on a Saturday in January, 1951, I took the=20
train to Washington and headed for GEICO's downtown headquarters.=20
To my dismay, the building was closed, but I pounded on the door=20
until a custodian appeared.  I asked this puzzled fellow if there=20
was anyone in the office I could talk to, and he said he'd seen=20
one man working on the sixth floor.

     And thus I met Lorimer Davidson, Assistant to the President,=20
who was later to become CEO.  Though my only credentials were=20
that I was a student of Graham's, "Davy" graciously spent four=20
hours or so showering me with both kindness and instruction.  No=20
one has ever received a better half-day course in how the=20
insurance industry functions nor in the factors that enable one=20
company to excel over others.  As Davy made clear, GEICO's method=20
of selling - direct marketing - gave it an enormous cost=20
advantage over competitors that sold through agents, a form of=20
distribution so ingrained in the business of these insurers that=20
it was impossible for them to give it up.  After my session with=20
Davy, I was more excited about GEICO than I have ever been about=20
a stock.

     When I finished at Columbia some months later and returned=20
to Omaha to sell securities, I naturally focused almost=20
exclusively on GEICO.  My first sales call - on my Aunt Alice,=20
who always supported me 100% - was successful.  But I was then a=20
skinny, unpolished 20-year-old who looked about 17, and my pitch=20
usually failed.  Undaunted, I wrote a short report late in 1951=20
about GEICO for "The Security I Like Best" column in The=20
<i>Commercial and Financial Chronicle,</i> a leading financial=20
publication of the time.  More important, I bought stock for my=20
own account.

     You may think this odd, but I have kept copies of every tax=20
return I filed, starting with the return for 1944.  Checking=20
back, I find that I purchased GEICO shares on four occasions=20
during 1951, the last purchase being made on September 26.  This=20
pattern of persistence suggests to me that my tendency toward=20
self-intoxication was developed early.  I probably came back on=20
that September day from unsuccessfully trying to sell some=20
prospect and decided - despite my already having more than 50% of=20
my net worth in GEICO - to load up further.  In any event, I=20
accumulated 350 shares of GEICO during the year, at a cost of=20
$10,282.  At yearend, this holding was worth $13,125, more than=20
65% of my net worth.

     You can see why GEICO was my first business love.  Furthermore,=20
just to complete this stroll down memory lane, I should add=20
that I earned most of the funds I used to buy GEICO shares by=20
delivering <i>The Washington Post,</i> the chief product of a=20
company that much later made it possible for Berkshire to turn=20
$10 million into $500 million.

     Alas, I sold my entire GEICO position in 1952 for $15,259,=20
primarily to switch into Western Insurance Securities.  This act=20
of infidelity can partially be excused by the fact that Western=20
was selling for slightly more than one times its current earnings,=20
a p/e ratio that for some reason caught my eye.  But in the next=20
20 years, the GEICO stock I sold grew in value to about $1.3=20
million, which taught me a lesson about the inadvisability of=20
selling a stake in an identifiably-wonderful company.

     In the early 1970's, after Davy retired, the executives=20
running GEICO made some serious errors in estimating their claims=20
costs, a mistake that led the company to underprice its policies=20
- and that almost caused it to go bankrupt.  The company was=20
saved only because Jack Byrne came in as CEO in 1976 and took=20
drastic remedial measures.

     Because I believed both in Jack and in GEICO's fundamental=20
competitive strength, Berkshire purchased a large interest in the=20
company during the second half of 1976, and also made smaller=20
purchases later.  By yearend 1980, we had put $45.7 million into=20
GEICO and owned 33.3% of its shares.  During the next 15 years,=20
we did not make further purchases.  Our interest in the company,=20
nonetheless, grew to about 50% because it was a big repurchaser=20
of its own shares.

     Then, in 1995, we agreed to pay $2.3 billion for the half of=20
the company we didn't own.  That is a steep price.  But it gives=20
us full ownership of a growing enterprise whose business remains=20
exceptional for precisely the same reasons that prevailed in=20
1951.  In addition, GEICO has two extraordinary managers:  Tony=20
Nicely, who runs the insurance side of the operation, and Lou=20
Simpson, who runs investments.

     Tony, 52, has been with GEICO for 34 years.  There's no one=20
I would rather have managing GEICO's insurance operation.  He has=20
brains, energy, integrity and focus.  If we're lucky, he'll stay=20
another 34 years.

     Lou runs investments just as ably.  Between 1980 and 1995,=20
the equities under Lou's management returned an average of 22.8%=20
annually vs. 15.7% for the S&amp;P.  Lou takes the same conservative,=20
concentrated approach to investments that we do at Berkshire, and=20
it is an enormous plus for us to have him on board.  One point=20
that goes beyond Lou's GEICO work:  His presence on the scene=20
assures us that Berkshire would have an extraordinary=20
professional immediately available to handle its investments if=20
something were to happen to Charlie and me.

     GEICO, of course, must continue both to attract good=20
policyholders and keep them happy.  It must also reserve and=20
price properly.  But the ultimate key to the company's success is=20
its rock-bottom operating costs, which virtually no competitor=20
can match.  In 1995, moreover, Tony and his management team=20
pushed underwriting and loss adjustment expenses down further to=20
23.6% of premiums, nearly one percentage point below 1994's=20
ratio.  In business, I look for economic castles protected by=20
unbreachable "moats."  Thanks to Tony and his management team,=20
GEICO's moat widened in 1995.

     Finally, let me bring you up to date on Davy.  He's now 93=20
and remains my friend and teacher.  He continues to pay close=20
attention to GEICO and has always been there when the company's=20
CEOs - Jack Byrne, Bill Snyder and Tony - have needed him.  Our=20
acquisition of 100% of GEICO caused Davy to incur a large tax. =20
Characteristically, he still warmly supported the transaction.

     Davy has been one of my heroes for the 45 years I've known=20
him, and he's never let me down.  You should understand that=20
Berkshire would not be where it is today if Davy had not been so=20
generous with his time on a cold Saturday in 1951.  I've often=20
thanked him privately, but it is fitting that I use this report=20
to thank him on behalf of Berkshire's shareholders.

<b>Insurance Operations</b>

     In addition to acquiring GEICO, we enjoyed other favorable=20
developments in insurance during 1995.

     As we've explained in past reports, what counts in our=20
insurance business is, first, the amount of "float" we generate=20
and, second, its cost to us.  Float is money we hold but don't=20
own.  In an insurance operation, float arises because most=20
policies require that premiums be prepaid and, more importantly,=20
because it usually takes time for an insurer to hear about and=20
resolve loss claims.

     Typically, the premiums that an insurer takes in do not=20
cover the losses and expenses it must pay.  That leaves it=20
running an "underwriting loss" - and that loss is the cost of=20
float.  An insurance business is profitable over time if its cost=20
of float is less than the cost the company would otherwise incur=20
to obtain funds.  But the business has a negative value if the=20
cost of its float is higher than market rates for money.

     As the numbers in the following table show, Berkshire's=20
insurance business has been a huge winner.  For the table, we=20
have calculated our float -  which we generate in exceptional=20
amounts relative to our premium volume - by adding loss reserves,=20
loss adjustment reserves, funds held under reinsurance assumed=20
and unearned premium reserves, and then subtracting agents'=20
balances, prepaid acquisition costs, prepaid taxes and deferred=20
charges applicable to assumed reinsurance.  Our cost of float is=20
determined by our underwriting loss or profit.  In those years=20
when we have had an underwriting profit, such as the last three,=20
our cost of float has been negative, which means we have=20
calculated our insurance earnings by adding underwriting profit=20
to float income.

