     Since 1967, when we entered the insurance business, our float=20
has grown at an annual compounded rate of 20.7%.  In more years=20
than not, our cost of funds has been less than nothing.  This=20
access to "free" money has boosted Berkshire's performance in a=20
major way.

     Any company's level of profitability is determined by three=20
items:  (1) what its assets earn; (2) what its liabilities cost;=20
and (3) its utilization of "leverage" - that is, the degree to=20
which its assets are funded by liabilities rather than by equity. =20
Over the years, we have done well on Point 1, having produced high=20
returns on our assets.  But we have also benefitted greatly - to a=20
degree that is not generally well-understood - because our=20
liabilities have cost us very little.  An important reason for this=20
low cost is that we have obtained float on very advantageous terms.=20
The same cannot be said by many other property and casualty=20
insurers, who may generate plenty of float, but at a cost that=20
exceeds what the funds are worth to them.  In those circumstances,=20
leverage becomes a disadvantage.

     Since our float has cost us virtually nothing over the years,=20
it has in effect served as equity.  Of course, it differs from true=20
equity in that it doesn't belong to us.  Nevertheless, let's assume=20
that instead of our having $3.4 billion of float at the end of=20
1994, we had replaced it with $3.4 billion of equity.  Under this=20
scenario, we would have owned no more assets than we did during=20
1995.  We would, however, have had somewhat lower earnings because=20
the cost of float was negative last year.  That is, our float threw=20
off profits.  And, of course, to obtain the replacement equity, we=20
would have needed to sell many new shares of Berkshire.  The net=20
result - more shares, equal assets and lower earnings - would have=20
materially reduced the value of our stock.  So you can understand=20
why float wonderfully benefits a business - <i>if</i> it is obtained at a=
=20
low cost.

     Our acquisition of GEICO will immediately increase our float=20
by nearly $3 billion, with additional growth almost certain.  We=20
also expect GEICO to operate at a decent underwriting profit in=20
most years, a fact that will increase the probability that our=20
total float will cost us nothing.  Of course, we paid a very=20
substantial price for the GEICO float, whereas virtually all of the=20
gains in float depicted in the table were developed internally.

     Our enthusiasm over 1995's insurance results must be tempered=20
once again because we had our third straight year of good fortune=20
in the super-cat business.  In this operation, we sell policies=20
that insurance and reinsurance companies buy to protect themselves=20
from the effects of mega-catastrophes.  Since truly major=20
catastrophes occur infrequently, our super-cat business can be=20
expected to show large profits in most years but occasionally to=20
record a huge loss.  In other words, the attractiveness of our=20
super-cat business will take many years to measure.  We know that=20
the results of years like the past three will be at least partially=20
offset by some truly terrible year in the future.  We just hope=20
that "partially" turns out to be the proper adverb.

     There were plenty of catastrophes last year, but no super-cats=20
of the insured variety.  The Southeast had a close call when Opal,=20
sporting winds of 150 miles per hour, hovered off Florida. =20
However, the storm abated before hitting land, and so a second=20
Andrew was dodged.  For insurers, the Kobe earthquake was another=20
close call:  The economic damage was huge - perhaps even a record -=20
but only a tiny portion of it was insured.  The insurance industry=20
won't always be that lucky.

     Ajit Jain is the guiding genius of our super-cat business and=20
writes important non-cat business as well. In insurance, the term=20
"catastrophe" is applied to an event, such as a hurricane or=20
earthquake, that causes a great many insured losses. The other=20
deals Ajit enters into usually cover only a single large loss. A=20
simplified description of three transactions from last year will=20
illustrate both what I mean and Ajit's versatility. We insured: (1)=20
The life of Mike Tyson for a sum that is large initially and that,=20
fight-by-fight, gradually declines to zero over the next few years;=20
(2) Lloyd's against more than 225 of its "names" dying during the=20
year; and (3) The launch, and a year of orbit, of two Chinese=20
satellites. Happily, both satellites are orbiting, the Lloyd's folk=20
avoided abnormal mortality, and if Mike Tyson looked any healthier,=20
no one would get in the ring with him.

     Berkshire is sought out for many kinds of insurance, both=20
super-cat and large single-risk, because: (1) our financial=20
strength is unmatched, and insureds know we can and will pay our=20
losses under the most adverse of circumstances; (2) we can supply a=20
quote faster than anyone in the business; and (3) we will issue=20
policies with limits larger than anyone else is prepared to write.=20
Most of our competitors have extensive reinsurance treaties and=20
lay off much of their business.  While this helps them avoid shock=20
losses, it also hurts their flexibility and reaction time.  As you=20
know, Berkshire moves quickly to seize investment and acquisition=20
opportunities; in insurance we respond with the same exceptional=20
speed.  In another important point, large coverages don't frighten=20
us but, on the contrary, intensify our interest.  We have offered a=20
<i>policy</i> under which we could have lost $1 billion; the largest=20
coverage that a client <i>accepted</i> was $400 million.

     We will get hit from time to time with large losses.  Charlie=20
and I, however, are quite willing to accept relatively volatile=20
results in exchange for better long-term earnings than we would=20
otherwise have had.  In other words, we prefer a lumpy 15% to a=20
smooth 12%.  Since most managers opt for smoothness, we are left=20
with a competitive advantage that we try to maximize.  We do,=20
though, monitor our aggregate exposure in order to keep our "worst=20
case" at a level that leaves us comfortable.

     Indeed, our worst case from a "once-in-a-century" super-cat is=20
far less severe - relative to net worth - than that faced by many=20
well-known primary companies writing great numbers of property=20
policies.  These insurers don't issue single huge-limit policies as=20
we do, but their small policies, in aggregate, can create a risk of=20
staggering size.  The "big one" would blow right through the=20
reinsurance covers of some of these insurers, exposing them to=20
uncapped losses that could threaten their survival.  In our case,=20
losses would be large, but capped at levels we could easily handle.

     Prices are weakening in the super-cat field.  That is=20
understandable considering the influx of capital into the=20
reinsurance business a few years ago and the natural desire of=20
those holding the capital to employ it.  No matter what others may=20
do, we will not knowingly write business at inadequate rates.  We=20
unwittingly did this in the early 1970's and, after more than 20=20
years, regularly receive significant bills stemming from the=20
mistakes of that era.  My guess is that we will still be getting=20
surprises from that business 20 years from now.  A bad reinsurance=20
contract is like hell:  easy to enter and impossible to exit.

     I actively participated in those early reinsurance decisions,=20
and Berkshire paid a heavy tuition for my education in the=20
business.  Unfortunately, reinsurance students can't attend school=20
on scholarship.  GEICO, incidentally, suffered a similar,=20
disastrous experience in the early 1980's, when it plunged=20
enthusiastically into the writing of reinsurance and large risks. =20
GEICO's folly was brief, but it will be cleaning things up for at=20
least another decade.  The well-publicized problems at Lloyd's=20
further illustrate the perils of reinsurance and also underscore=20
how vital it is that the interests of the people who write=20
insurance business be aligned - on the downside as well as the=20
upside - with those of the people putting up the capital.  When=20
that kind of symmetry is missing, insurers almost invariably run=20
into trouble, though its existence may remain hidden for some time.

     A small, apocryphal story about an insurance CEO who was=20
visited by an analyst tells a lot about this industry.  To the=20
analyst's questions about his business, the CEO had nothing but=20
gloomy answers:  Rates were ridiculously low; the reserves on his=20
balance sheet weren't adequate for ordinary claims, much less those=20
likely to arise from asbestos and environmental problems; most of=20
his reinsurers had long since gone broke, leaving him holding the=20
sack.  But then the CEO brightened:  "Still, things could be a lot=20
worse," he said.  "It could be <i>my</i> money."  At Berkshire, it's <i>our=
</i>=20
money.

     Berkshire's other insurance operations, though relatively=20
small, performed magnificently in 1995.  National Indemnity's=20
traditional business had a combined ratio of 84.2 and developed, as=20
usual, a large amount of float compared to premium volume.  Over=20
the last three years, this segment of our business, run by Don=20
Wurster, has had an average combined ratio of 85.6.  Our homestate=20
operation, managed by Rod Eldred, grew at a good rate in 1995 and=20
achieved a combined ratio of 81.4.  Its three-year combined ratio=20
is an amazing 82.4.  Berkshire's California workers' compensation=20
business, run by Brad Kinstler, faced fierce price-cutting in 1995=20
and lost a great many renewals when we refused to accept inadequate=20
rates.  Though this operation's volume dropped materially, it=20
produced an excellent underwriting profit.  Finally, John Kizer, at=20
Central States Indemnity, continues to do an extraordinary job. =20
His premium volume was up 23% in 1995, and underwriting profit grew=20
by 59%.  Ajit, Don, Rod, Brad and John are all under 45, an=20
embarrassing fact demolishing my theory that managers only hit=20
their stride after they reach 70.

     To sum up, we entered 1995 with an exceptional insurance=20
operation of moderate size.  By adding GEICO, we entered 1996 with=20
a business still better in quality, much improved in its growth=20
prospects, and doubled in size.  More than ever, insurance is our=20
core strength.

