     A large amount of information about these businesses is given=20
on pages 41-52, where you will also find our segment earnings=20
reported on a GAAP basis.  In addition, on pages 57-63, we have=20
rearranged Berkshire's financial data into four segments on a non-
GAAP basis, a presentation that corresponds to the way Charlie and=20
I think about the company.  Our intent is to supply you with the=20
financial information that we would wish you to give us if our=20
positions were reversed.

     At Berkshire, we believe in Charlie's dictum - "Just tell me=20
the bad news; the good news will take care of itself" - and that is=20
the behavior we expect of our managers when they are reporting to=20
us.  Consequently, I also owe you - Berkshire's owners - a report=20
on three operations that, though they continued to earn decent (or=20
better) returns on invested capital, experienced a decline in=20
earnings last year.  Each encountered a different type of problem.

     Our shoe business operated in an industry that suffered=20
depressed earnings throughout last year, and many of our=20
competitors made only marginal profits or worse.  That means we at=20
least maintained, and in some instances widened, our competitive=20
superiority.  So I have no doubt that our shoe operations will=20
climb back to top-grade earnings in the future.  In other words,=20
though the turn has not yet occurred, we believe you should view=20
last year's figures as reflecting a cyclical problem, not a secular=20
one.

     The Buffalo News, though still doing very well in comparison=20
to other newspapers, is another story.  In this case, industry=20
trends are not good.  In the 1991 Annual Report, I explained that=20
newspapers had lost a notch in their economic attractiveness from=20
the days when they appeared to have a bullet-proof franchise. =20
Today, the industry retains its excellent economics, but has lost=20
still another notch.  Over time, we expect the competitive strength=20
of newspapers to gradually erode, though the industry should=20
nevertheless remain a fine business for many years to come.

     Berkshire's most difficult problem is World Book, which=20
operates in an industry beset by increasingly tough competition=20
from CD-ROM and on-line offerings.  True, we are still profitable,=20
a claim that perhaps no other print encyclopedia can make.  But our=20
sales and earnings trends have gone in the wrong direction.  At the=20
end of 1995, World Book made major changes in the way it=20
distributes its product, stepped up its efforts with electronic=20
products and sharply reduced its overhead costs.  It will take time=20
for us to evaluate the effects of these initiatives, but we are=20
confident they will significantly improve our viability.=20

     All of our operations, including those whose earnings fell=20
last year, benefit from exceptionally talented and dedicated=20
managers.  Were we to have the choice of any other executives now=20
working in their industries, there is not one of our managers we=20
would replace.

     Many of our managers don't have to work for a living, but=20
simply go out and perform every day for the same reason that=20
wealthy golfers stay on the tour:  They love both doing what they=20
do and doing it well.  To describe them as working may be a=20
misnomer - they simply prefer spending much of their time on a=20
productive activity at which they excel to spending it on leisure=20
activities.  Our job is to provide an environment that will keep=20
them feeling this way, and so far we seem to have succeeded: =20
Thinking back over the 1965-95 period, I can't recall that a single=20
key manager has left Berkshire to join another employer.

