     Our best holding has been Gillette, which we told you from the=20
start was a superior business.  Ironically, though, this is also=20
the purchase in which I made my biggest mistake - of a kind,=20
however, never recognized on financial statements.

     We paid $600 million in 1989 for Gillette preferred shares=20
that were convertible into 48 million (split-adjusted) common=20
shares.  Taking an alternative route with the $600 million, I=20
probably could have purchased 60 million shares of common from the=20
company.  The market on the common was then about $10.50, and given=20
that this would have been a huge private placement carrying=20
important restrictions, I probably could have bought the stock at a=20
discount of at least 5%.  I can't be sure about this, but it's=20
likely that Gillette's management would have been just as happy to=20
have Berkshire opt for common.

     But I was far too clever to do that.  Instead, for less than=20
two years, we received some extra dividend income (the difference=20
between the preferred's yield and that of the common), at which=20
point the company - quite properly - called the issue, moving to do=20
that as quickly as was possible.  If I had negotiated for common=20
rather than preferred, we would have been better off at yearend=20
1995 by $625 million, minus the "excess" dividends of about $70=20
million.

     In the case of Champion, the ability of the company to call=20
our preferred at 115% of cost forced a move out of us last August=20
that we would rather have delayed.  In this instance, we converted=20
our shares just prior to the pending call and offered them to the=20
company at a modest discount.

     Charlie and I have never had a conviction about the paper=20
industry - actually, I can't remember ever owning the common stock=20
of a paper producer in my 54 years of investing - so our choice in=20
August was whether to sell in the market or to the company. =20
Champion's management had always been candid and honorable in=20
dealing with us and wished to repurchase common shares, so we=20
offered our stock to the company.  Our Champion capital gain was=20
moderate - about 19% after tax from a six-year investment - but the=20
preferred delivered us a good after-tax dividend yield throughout=20
our holding period.  (That said, many press accounts have=20
overstated the after-tax yields earned by property-casualty=20
insurance companies on dividends paid to them.  What the press has=20
failed to take into account is a change in the tax law that took=20
effect in 1987 and that significantly reduced the dividends=20
received credit applicable to insurers.  For details, see our 1986=20
Annual Report.)

     Our First Empire preferred will be called on March 31, 1996,=20
the earliest date allowable.  We are comfortable owning stock in=20
well-run banks, and we will convert and keep our First Empire=20
common shares.  Bob Wilmers, CEO of the company, is an outstanding=20
banker, and we love being associated with him.

     Our other two preferreds have been disappointing, though the=20
Salomon preferred has modestly outperformed the fixed-income=20
securities for which it was a substitute.  However, the amount of=20
management time Charlie and I have devoted to this holding has been=20
vastly greater than its economic significance to Berkshire. =20
Certainly I never dreamed I would take a new job at age 60 -=20
Salomon interim chairman, that is - because of an earlier purchase=20
of a fixed-income security.

     Soon after our purchase of the Salomon preferred in 1987, I=20
wrote that I had "no special insights regarding the direction or=20
future profitability of investment banking."  Even the most=20
charitable commentator would conclude that I have since proved my=20
point.

     To date, our option to convert into Salomon common has not=20
proven of value.  Furthermore, the Dow Industrials have doubled=20
since I committed to buy the preferred, and the brokerage group has=20
performed equally as well.  That means my decision to go with=20
Salomon because I saw value in the conversion option must be graded=20
as very poor.  Even so, the preferred has continued under some=20
trying conditions to deliver as a fixed-income security, and the=20
9% dividend is currently quite attractive.

     Unless the preferred is converted, its terms require=20
redemption of 20% of the issue on October 31 of each year, 1995-99,=20
and $140 million of our original $700 million was taken on schedule=20
last year.  (Some press reports labeled this a sale, but a senior=20
security that matures is not "sold.")  Though we did not elect to=20
convert the preferred that matured last year, we have four more=20
bites at the conversion apple, and I believe it quite likely that=20
we will yet find value in our right to convert.

     I discussed the USAir investment at length in last year's=20
report.  The company's results improved in 1995, but it still faces=20
significant problems.  On the plus side for us is the fact that our=20
preferred is structurally well-designed:  For example, though we=20
have not been paid dividends since June 1994, the amounts owed us=20
are compounding at 5% over the prime rate.  On the minus side is=20
the fact that we are dealing with a weak credit.

     We feel much better about our USAir preferred than we did a=20
year ago, but your guess is as good as mine as to its ultimate=20
value.  (Indeed, considering my record with this investment, it's=20
fair to say that your guess may be <i>better</i> than mine.)  At yearend=20
we carried our preferred (in which there is no public market) at=20
60% of par, though USAir also has outstanding a junior preferred=20
that is significantly inferior to ours in all respects except=20
conversion price and that was then trading at 82% of par.  As I=20
write this, the junior issue has advanced to 97% of par.  Let's=20
hope the market is right.

     Overall, our preferreds have performed well, but that is true=20
only because of one huge winner, Gillette.  Leaving aside Gillette,=20
our preferreds as a group have delivered us after-tax returns no=20
more than equal to those we could have earned from the medium-term=20
fixed-income issues that they replaced.

<b>A Proposed Recapitalization</b>

     At the Annual Meeting you will be asked to approve a=20
recapitalization of Berkshire, creating two classes of stock.  If=20
the plan is adopted, our existing common stock will be designated=20
as Class A Common Stock and a new Class B Common Stock will be=20
authorized.

     Each share of the "B" will have the rights of 1/30th of an "A"=20
share with these exceptions:  First, a B share will have 1/200th of=20
the vote of an A share (rather than 1/30th of the vote).  Second,=20
the B will not be eligible to participate in Berkshire's=20
shareholder-designated charitable contributions program.

     When the recapitalization is complete, each share of A will=20
become convertible, at the holder's option and at any time, into 30=20
shares of B.  This conversion privilege will not extend in the=20
opposite direction.  That is, holders of B shares will not be able=20
to convert them into A shares.

     We expect to list the B shares on the New York Stock Exchange,=20
where they will trade alongside the A stock.  To create the=20
shareholder base necessary for a listing - and to ensure a liquid=20
market in the B stock - Berkshire expects to make a public offering=20
for cash of at least $100 million of new B shares.  The offering=20
will be made only by means of a prospectus.

     The market will ultimately determine the price of the B=20
shares.  Their price, though, should be in the neighborhood of=20
1/30th of the price of the A shares.

     Class A shareholders who wish to give gifts may find it=20
convenient to convert a share or two of their stock into Class B=20
shares.  Additionally, arbitrage-related conversions will occur if=20
demand for the B is strong enough to push its price to slightly=20
above 1/30th of the price of A.

     However, because the Class A stock will entitle its holders to=20
full voting rights and access to Berkshire's contributions program,=20
these shares will be superior to the Class B shares and we would=20
expect most shareholders to remain holders of the Class A - which=20
is precisely what the Buffett and Munger families plan to do,=20
except in those instances when we ourselves might convert a few=20
shares to facilitate gifts.  The prospect that most shareholders=20
will stick to the A stock suggests that it will enjoy a somewhat=20
more liquid market than the B.

     There are tradeoffs for Berkshire in this recapitalization. =20
But they do not arise from the proceeds of the offering - we will=20
find constructive uses for the money - nor in any degree from the=20
price at which we will sell the B shares.  As I write this - with=20
Berkshire stock at $36,000 - Charlie and I do not believe it=20
undervalued.  Therefore, the offering we propose will not diminish=20
the per-share intrinsic value of our existing stock.  Let me also=20
put our thoughts about valuation more baldly:  Berkshire is selling=20
at a price at which Charlie and I would not consider buying it.

     What Berkshire will incur by way of the B stock are certain=20
added costs, including those involving the mechanics of handling a=20
larger number of shareholders.  On the other hand, the stock should=20
be a convenience for people wishing to make gifts.  And those of=20
you who have hoped for a split have gained a do-it-yourself method=20
of bringing one about.

     We are making this move, though, for other reasons - having to=20
do with the appearance of expense-laden unit trusts purporting to=20
be low-priced "clones" of Berkshire and sure to be aggressively=20
marketed.  The idea behind these vehicles is not new:  In recent=20
years, a number of people have told me about their wish to create=20
an "all-Berkshire" investment fund to be sold at a low dollar=20
price.  But until recently, the promoters of these investments=20
heard out my objections and backed off.

     I did not discourage these people because I prefer large=20
investors over small.  Were it possible, Charlie and I would love=20
to turn $1,000 into $3,000 for multitudes of people who would find=20
that gain an important answer to their immediate problems.

     In order to quickly triple small stakes, however, we would=20
have to just as quickly turn our present market capitalization of=20
$43 billion into $129 billion (roughly the market cap of General=20
Electric, America's most highly valued company).  <i>We can't come=20
close to doing that.</i> The very best we hope for is - on average - to=20
double Berkshire's per-share intrinsic value every five years, and=20
we may well fall far short of that goal.

     In the end, Charlie and I do not care whether our shareholders=20
own Berkshire in large or small amounts.  What we wish for are=20
shareholders of any size who are knowledgeable about our=20
operations, share our objectives and long-term perspective, and are=20
aware of our limitations, most particularly those imposed by our=20
large capital base.

     The unit trusts that have recently surfaced fly in the face of=20
these goals.  They would be sold by brokers working for big=20
commissions, would impose other burdensome costs on their=20
shareholders, and would be marketed <i>en masse</i> to unsophisticated=20
buyers, apt to be seduced by our past record and beguiled by the=20
publicity Berkshire and I have received in recent years.  The sure=20
outcome:  a multitude of investors destined to be disappointed.

     Through our creation of the B stock - a low-denomination=20
product far superior to Berkshire-only trusts - we hope to make the=20
clones unmerchandisable.

     But both present and prospective Berkshire shareholders should=20
pay special attention to one point:  Though the per-share intrinsic=20
value of our stock has grown at an excellent rate during the past=20
five years, its market price has grown still faster.  The stock, in=20
other words, has outperformed the business.

     That kind of market overperformance cannot persist indefinitely,=20
neither for Berkshire nor any other stock.  <i>Inevitably, there=20
will be periods of underperformance as well.</i>  The price=20
volatility that results, though endemic to public markets, is=20
not to our liking.  What we would prefer instead is to have the=20
market price of Berkshire precisely track its intrinsic value. =20
Were the stock to do that, every shareholder would benefit during=20
his period of ownership in exact proportion to the progress=20
Berkshire itself made in the period.

     Obviously, the market behavior of Berkshire's stock will never=20
conform to this ideal.  But we will come closer to this goal than=20
we would otherwise if our present and prospective shareholders are=20
informed, business-oriented and not exposed to high-commission=20
salesmanship when making their investment decisions.  To that end,=20
we are better off if we can blunt the merchandising efforts of the=20
unit trusts - and that is the reason we are creating the B stock.

     We look forward to answering your questions about the=20
recapitalization at the Annual Meeting.

<b>Miscellaneous</b>

     Berkshire isn't the only American corporation utilizing the=20
new, exciting ABWA strategy.  At about 1:15 p.m. on July 14, 1995,=20
Michael Eisner, CEO of The Walt Disney Company, was walking up=20
Wildflower Lane in Sun Valley.  At the same time, I was leaving a=20
lunch at Herbert Allen's home on that street to meet Tom Murphy,=20
CEO of Cap Cities/ABC, for a golf game.

     That morning, speaking to a large group of executives and=20
money managers assembled by Allen's investment bank, Michael had=20
made a brilliant presentation about Disney, and upon seeing him, I=20
offered my congratulations.  We chatted briefly - and the subject=20
of a possible combination of Disney and Cap Cities came up.  This=20
wasn't the first time a merger had been discussed, but progress had=20
never before been made, in part because Disney wanted to buy with=20
cash and Cap Cities desired stock.

     Michael and I waited a few minutes for Murph to arrive, and in=20
the short conversation that ensued, both Michael and Murph=20
indicated they might bend on the stock/cash question.  Within a few=20
weeks, they both did, at which point a contract was put together in=20
three very busy days.

     The Disney/Cap Cities deal makes so much sense that I'm sure=20
it would have occurred without that chance encounter in Sun Valley.=20
But when I ran into Michael that day on Wildflower Lane, he was=20
heading for his plane, so without that accidental meeting the deal=20
certainly wouldn't have happened in the time frame it did.  I=20
believe both Disney and Cap Cities will benefit from the fact that=20
we all serendipitously met that day.

                      * * * * * * * * * * * *

     It's appropriate that I say a few words here about Murph.  To=20
put it simply, he is as fine an executive as I have ever seen in my=20
long exposure to business.  Equally important, he possesses human=20
qualities every bit the equal of his managerial qualities.  He's an=20
extraordinary friend, parent, husband and citizen.  In those rare=20
instances in which Murph's personal interests diverged from those=20
of shareholders, he unfailingly favored the owners.  When I say=20
that I like to be associated with managers whom I would love to=20
have as a sibling, in-law, or trustee of my will, Murph is the=20
exemplar of what I mean.

     If Murph should elect to run another business, don't bother to=20
study its value - just buy the stock.  And don't later be as dumb=20
as I was two years ago when I sold one-third of our holdings in Cap=20
Cities for $635 million (versus the $1.27 billion those shares=20
would bring in the Disney merger).

