     When it comes to our Annual Meetings, Charlie and I are=20
managerial oddballs:  We thoroughly enjoy the event.  So come join=20
us on Monday, May 6.  At Berkshire, we have no investor relations=20
department and don't use financial analysts as a channel for=20
disseminating information, earnings "guidance," or the like. =20
Instead, we prefer direct manager-to-owner communication and=20
believe that the Annual Meeting is the ideal place for this=20
interchange of ideas.  Talking to you there is efficient for us and=20
also democratic in that all present simultaneously hear what we=20
have to say.

     Last year, for the first time, we had the Annual Meeting at=20
the Holiday Convention Centre and the logistics seemed to work. =20
The ballroom there was filled with about 3,200 people, and we had a=20
video feed into a second room holding another 800 people.  Seating=20
in the main room was a little tight, so this year we will probably=20
configure it to hold 3,000.  This year we will also have two rooms=20
for the overflow.

     All in all, we will be able to handle 5,000 shareholders.  The=20
meeting will start at 9:30 a.m., but be warned that last year the=20
main ballroom was filled shortly after 8:00 a.m.

     Shareholders from 49 states attended our 1995 meeting - where=20
were you, Vermont? - and a number of foreign countries, including=20
Australia, Sweden and Germany, were represented.  As always, the=20
meeting attracted shareholders who were interested in Berkshire's=20
business - as contrasted to shareholders who are primarily=20
interested in themselves - and the questions were all good. =20
Charlie and I ate lunch on stage and answered questions for about=20
five hours.

     We feel that if owners come from all over the world, we should=20
try to make sure they have an opportunity to ask their questions. =20
Most shareholders leave about noon, but a thousand or so hardcore=20
types usually stay to see whether we will drop.  Charlie and I are=20
in training to last at least five hours again this year.

     We will have our usual array of Berkshire products at the=20
meeting and this year will add a sales representative from GEICO. =20
At the 1995 meeting, we sold 747 pounds of candy, 759 pairs of=20
shoes, and over $17,500 of World Books and related publications. =20
In a move that might have been dangerous had our stock been weak,=20
we added knives last year from our Quikut subsidiary and sold 400=20
sets of these.  (We draw the line at soft fruit, however.)  All of=20
these goods will again be available this year.  We don't consider a=20
cultural event complete unless a little business is mixed in.

     Because we expect a large crowd for the meeting, we recommend=20
that you promptly get both plane and hotel reservations.  Those of=20
you who like to be downtown (about six miles from the Centre) may=20
wish to stay at the Radisson Redick Tower, a small (88 rooms) but=20
nice hotel, or at the much larger Red Lion Hotel a few blocks away.=20
In the vicinity of the Centre are the Holiday Inn (403 rooms),=20
Homewood Suites (118 rooms) and Hampton Inn (136 rooms).  Another=20
recommended spot is the Marriott, whose west Omaha location is=20
about 100 yards from Borsheim's and a ten-minute drive from the=20
Centre.  There will be buses at the Marriott that will leave at=20
7:30, 8:00 and 8:30 for the meeting and return after it ends.

     An attachment to our proxy material explains how you can=20
obtain the card you will need for admission to the meeting.  A=20
good-sized parking area is available at the Centre, while those who=20
stay at the Holiday Inn, Homewood Suites and Hampton Inn will be=20
